{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Concurrency Features"}, {"type": "position", "content": 8}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are some words that you can use to talk about concurrency features."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange written and spoken information on principles, concepts, data base objects related to query analysis, and data base engines.  Describe relational database, its concepts, and principles in spoken or written texts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses information a wider range of contexts about exchanging technical information on software programming and software solutions, using appropriate grammar and vocabulary at this level.   Recognizes simple ideas on concepts on software programming, software solutions and database management."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Be Supposed To in Present, Past, and Future"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use <strong>be supposed to</strong> in any tense. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They will always be supposed to implement proper controls, otherwise, data could be updated or changed improperly, thus generating a serious impact on the system."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Present Tense</b></br> Use <strong>be supposed to</strong> in present to talk about actions that people are expecting from you, or that are always true"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Structure</br> Subject + am/is/are + supposed to + verb + complement"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples:</br><i>I <b>am supposed to</b> wait for a turn to manage data concurrency.</br> Users <b>are supposed to</b> wait for a turn to manage data concurrency.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Past Tense</b></br> Use<strong> be supposed to</strong> to talk about actions that people expected in a certain condition in the past but are not longer true"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structure</br> Subject + was/were + supposed to + verb + complement"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples:</br> <i>I <b>was supposed to</b> wait for a turn to manage data concurrency.</br> Decades ago, users <b>were supposed to</b> wait for a turn to manage data concurrency.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Future Tense</b></br> Use it to talk about actions people will expect from you when certain conditions are met. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structure</br> Subject + will be + supposed to + verb + complement"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:</br><i>As a user, you <b>will be supposed to</b> wait for a turn to manage data concurrency."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Are they supposed to be able to do all that? That sounds very difficult."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I was supposed to learn about that last semester."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Passive of Modal Verbs: Must"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the <b>passive voice</b> when we want to emphasize on the action and not on the person who does it. <b>Passive voice</b> can be used anytime with <b>modal verbs</b>; we are going to focus on <b>must</b> this time."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Important requirements must be followed. The data concurrency must be maximized, any data must be read and modified consistently because any change is very dangerous, and maximum productivity must always be achieved.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Must</strong> and <strong>Ought to</strong> are similar.</br> They are used to talk about obligations, but <strong>must</strong> is more commonly used than <strong>ought to</strong>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structure</br> Subject + must be + verb in past participle + complement"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:</br><i>Data <b>must be read</b> and <b>modified</b> consistently."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The word itself explains it all! It locks the data, since updates or deletes must be controlled to optimize productivity, locking lets you control the information that can be changed"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "consistently "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make something steadily without any change"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The data concurrency must be maximized, any data must be read and modified consistently because any change is very dangerous."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "concurrency"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the simultaneous access of many users to the same data"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They use concurrency, due to many users having access to the same data at the same time, they will always be supposed to implement proper controls."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "astonishing"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "surprising, striking"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Astonishing! That's exactly what it does, it creates private copies of the data, anyone can read or write it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "lock"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make secure something in order to avoid people having access to it"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The word itself explains it all! It locks the data, since updates or deletes must be controlled to optimize productivity."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "be up to "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to have the final decision about something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Locking lets you control the information that can be changed, it can be manual or automatic. It's up to you!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob08aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for more information about concurrency and consistency."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write pros and cons of those tools. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discus with my partners about concurrency a consistency."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I talk about concurrency and consistency."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I identify their pros and cons."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk to my partners about information system."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Data Concurrency and Consistency in a Multiuser Environment"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<b>Data concurrency and consistency</b> are mechanisms that allow users to access a database and modify the data while other users are updating the same data at the same time."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Decades ago, databases were supposed to be single-user databases, which means that users were supposed to wait for a turn to manage data in an individual way. Fortunately, databases have changed. Now, thanks to concurrency and consistency mechanisms, several users can update data and execute multiple simultaneous transactions in these multiuser databases. </br></br>Let's see a brief explanation of how data concurrency and data consistency work:</br></br><b>Data concurrency</b>: Allow many users to manage the same information at the same time.</br><b>Data consistency</b>: Allow users to see all changes in real time so that the database is always updated."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Michelle:</strong> I wonder how Oracle databases can handle so much information at the same time.</br><strong>Sara: </strong> Well, important requirements must be fulfilled. The data concurrency must be maximized; any data must be read and modified consistently because any improper change can be very dangerous, and maximum productivity must always be achieved.</br>\n<strong>Michelle: </strong> Are those databases supposed to be able to do all that? It sounds very difficult.</br>\n<strong>Sara: </strong> It is, but they have software mechanisms that help during the process. For example, they use concurrency, because many users have access to the same data at the same time. They will always be supposed to implement proper controls, otherwise, data could be updated or changed improperly, thus generating a serious impact on the system. They can also use read consistency.</br></div>"}, {"time": "53000", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong> Michelle</strong>: Wait a minute! I was supposed to learn about that last semester. What read consistency does is to make data available to be read or written, but without updating or changing the information for every single user, right?</br><strong> Sara</strong>: Astonishing! That's exactly what it does. It creates private copies of the data; anyone can read or write it, but the data at the database remain unchanged until they are needed.</br>\n<strong> Michelle</strong>: I have heard that catching and locking are also supposed to be used.</br></div>"}, {"time": "86400", "image": "enpe7n7le03ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Sara:</strong> Yeah, they use those, too. What catching allows is to create cache memory from user data, log data or even other types of data, and instead of repeating all processes, cache data is used whenever it is possible in order to save time.</br><strong> Michelle</strong>: And what about locking?</br><strong> Sara</strong>: The word itself explains it all! It locks the data since updates or deletes must be controlled to optimize productivity; locking allows you control the information that can be changed, it can be manual or automatic. It's up to you!</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le03ob08re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the requirements all information management systems must have."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss information about mechanisms like concurrency and read consistency. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about mechanisms like catching and locking."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think a database can allow several users to see and edit the same information at the same time? Why? Why not?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How can we recover original files in a database after people have made several changes to them?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attem